# {{ proper_name }}

## Build

    make            # build the web assets in src/ (runs npm install on first use)
    make run        # run from the source tree on http://127.0.0.1:8080/
    make test       # run the tests in tests/, needs pytest (pip install '.[test]')
    make install    # pip install the package

## Configuration

Settings are read from the `[{{ lower_name }}]` section of these ini files, in order:

- `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.ini.local`
- `/etc/{{ namespace or lower_name }}/{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- `~/.{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- any file passed with `--ini`
{% if 'auth' in features %}

## Logins

Every page needs a login. Add a user, or change their password, with:

    python3 -m {{ py_name }} --add-user <user>

`--remove-user <user>` removes a user and ends their sessions, and `--list-users` lists them.
Users are kept in `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.users`, and sessions, with the secret
that signs their cookies, in `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.sessions`, so logins survive
a restart. Set `users` and `sessions` in the `[auth]` section of the ini file to keep them
elsewhere, for example when the package is installed. `on_login_success()` in
`{{ py_path }}/{{ lower_name }}.py` decides what goes in a session.
{% end %}
{% if 'sqlite' in features %}

## Database

The app opens `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.db` at startup, or the `path` in the
`[sqlite]` section of the ini file, and creates the tables of the models in
`{{ py_path }}/models.py` that don't exist yet. Existing tables aren't changed, so a changed
model needs its table altered or dropped by hand. Handlers use the database with:

    async with db.session() as session:
        messages = await session.scalars(sqlalchemy.select(Message))

`/api/messages` is an example: GET lists the messages as JSON, and POST adds one from a body
like `{"text": "hi"}`.
{% end %}
{% if 'nginx' in features %}

## nginx

Install an nginx site that serves `/static/` itself and proxies the rest, including the
WebSocket, to the app:

    sudo python3 -m {{ py_name }} --nginx <server_name>
    sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/<server_name> /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
    sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx

It redirects HTTP to HTTPS and expects a certificate in `/etc/letsencrypt/live/<server_name>/`,
for example from `certbot certonly`. The `prefix` setting puts the app under a path, so other
sites can share the server. Settings in the `[nginx]` section of the ini file fill in
`{{ py_path }}/data/nginx.conf`.
{% end %}
{% if 'systemd' in features %}

## systemd

Install a service that runs the app from the current directory, then start it:

    sudo python3 -m {{ py_name }} --systemd [<user>]
    sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    sudo systemctl enable --now {{ lower_name }}

The service runs as `<user>`, or the user who ran `sudo`, with the same Python and
`--ini` file. Settings in the `[systemd]` section of the ini file fill in
`{{ py_path }}/data/systemd.service`.
{% end %}
